  "estimatedMinutes": 1,
  "statement": "Let a binary operation \\(*\\) on ordered pairs of integers be defined by \\((a,b)*(c,d)=(a-c,b+d).\\) Then, if \\((3,2)*(0,0)\\) and \\((x,y)*(3,2)\\) represent identical pairs, \\(x\\) equals:",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "\\(-3\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "\\(0\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "\\(2\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "\\(3\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "\\(6\\)"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "e",
    "value": "6"
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.ahsme-1969-p4.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "The first pair is \\((3-0,2+0)=(3,2).\\) The second is \\((x-3,y+2).\\) Equality of the first coordinates gives \\(x-3=3,\\) so \\(x=6.\\)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is E.",
      "hints": [
        "Evaluate each ordered-pair operation directly",
        "Equate the first coordinates \\(3\\) and \\(x-3\\)"
      ],
